   "product": "Colour from visible + near-infrared",
   "shows": "A morning pass (about 8:50 am local) over northeast Brazil. Green is land, white is cloud building over the interior, and on the right is the Atlantic, with the coast running from around Recife toward Salvador; the hazy bright patch on the sea is sunglint. The camera has no blue channel, so we built the colour from its visible (0.5\u20130.7 \u00b5m) and near-infrared (0.7\u20131.1 \u00b5m) channels: the greens are our mix, the shapes are real. About 1 km per pixel. The thin yellow line is the coastline placed from the satellite's orbit and timestamps; the black bars are moments the signal dropped. Tap for the full 2,800 km-wide strip.",
   "how": "Caught live at 11:45\u201311:57 UTC on 28 Oct 2026 through a volunteer's public RTL-SDR receiver with a simple quarter-wave VHF antenna in Bahia, Brazil; the satellite peaked at 59\u00b0. We streamed 12.5 minutes of raw I/Q (187,500 samples per second, 280 MB) with our own receiver client, then SatDump demodulated the 72k-symbol QPSK, ran Viterbi and Reed\u2013Solomon and rebuilt 776 scan lines, about 87% complete. We straightened the scan geometry, mixed the colour and laid the coastline on ourselves.",
