   "product": "Channel 4 \u00b7 thermal infrared",
   "shows": "A night pass (about 3:30 am local), so this is a heat picture, not a light one: the camera's 10.5\u201311.5 \u00b5m thermal channel sees temperature. Bright means cold (cloud tops, desert that cooled overnight), grey means warmer (the sea). The bright shape is the Baja California peninsula with the Gulf of California beside it, around 24\u201329\u00b0N. The thin yellow lines are coastlines placed from the satellite's orbit and the timestamps inside the signal; everything under them is our decode. Black bars are moments the signal dropped. Tap for the full 2,800 km-wide strip.",
   "how": "Caught live at 10:29\u201310:41 UTC on 28 Oct 2026 through a volunteer's public Airspy HF+ receiver with a 9-element FM antenna in Zapopan, near Guadalajara, Mexico; the satellite peaked at 34\u00b0. We streamed 11.5 minutes of raw I/Q (228,000 samples per second, 313 MB) with our own receiver client, then SatDump demodulated the 72k-symbol QPSK, ran Viterbi and Reed\u2013Solomon and rebuilt 568 scan lines, about 82% complete. We straightened the scan geometry and laid the coastlines on ourselves.",
