   "product": "Colour from visible + near-infrared",
   "shows": "A midday pass over western Europe, turned north-up. On the left is the Atlantic and France's straight west coast, then the French countryside dotted with small fair-weather clouds, and at bottom right a cloud blanket over Switzerland and the Alps. Only about 360 km of the pass came through (the signal was strong just around the overhead moment), but that slice is 97% complete. Same colour recipe as the Brazil photo (visible + near-infrared, no blue channel). Yellow lines are coastlines and borders placed from the orbit and timestamps. Tap for the full 2,800 km-wide strip.",
   "how": "Caught live at 13:22\u201313:35 UTC on 28 Oct 2026 through a volunteer's public Airspy HF+ receiver in Utrecht, Netherlands; the satellite peaked at 73\u00b0. We streamed 12.6 minutes of raw I/Q (192,000 samples per second, 291 MB) with our own receiver client, and SatDump rebuilt 360 scan lines from it. We straightened the geometry, mixed the colour and added the coastlines ourselves.",
